# Test hints for MultiService
# Multi-service example with HTTP, Socket, and File Monitor
type: multiservice
# mode: both — all three gaps that pinned this to the interpreter are closed.
#
# File events were the first: the compiled binary reported FILE MODIFIED for a
# creation on macOS and reported nothing at all on Linux, because only the
# FSEvents backend ever published an event — inotify and the polling fallback
# just printed a line. Fixed in GitLab #693.
#
# The socket welcome was the second: the compiled socket *server* dispatched to
# no `Socket Event Handler` at all, so `Handle Client Connected` never ran and
# nothing was sent. Fixed in GitLab #881.
#
# The third was GitLab #882: a compiled file monitor watched the process's
# working directory rather than the path it was given, so run from the project
# root it reported a FILE CREATED for every build artefact anything else
# happened to write —
#
#   Socket: FILE CREATED: ./.build/index-build/.../AROLSPTests~partial.swiftmodule.tmp
#
# — and passed only while the tree was quiet. The compiled binary delivers the
# path through `_expression_` where the interpreter uses `_literal_`, and only
# the latter was read; the former fell through to a `"."` default.
mode: both
timeout: 20
skip-on-windows: Multi-service tests not supported on Windows CI
